import axios from 'axios';

async function getTasks() {
  const response = await axios.get<Task[]>('http://localhost:3000/tasks.json');
  return response.data;
}

interface Task {
  id: number;
  title: string;
  completed: boolean;
}

getTasks().then(tasks => {
  console.log(tasks);
  // [
  //   { id: 1, title: 'Task 1', completed: false },
  //   { id: 2, title: 'Task 2', completed: true }
  // ] 
});
```

A few things to note:

- We use `axios.get<Task[]>` to specify the expected response data type. This will give us TypeScript type safety and autocompletion.
- We define an `interface` for the `Task` data.
- The response data is typed as `Task[]` (array of `Task` objects)
- We can access `response.data` with full type safety.

This allows us to consume a JSON API in TypeScript with a great developer experience - autocompletion, type safety, and catch typing errors at compile time.

